+# Cache-Control on deploy
+
+## What I changed
+
+`scripts/deploy.sh` now sets `Cache-Control` on every object it uploads:
+
+- `src/` except `assets/` (html, css, js): `public, max-age=300` (5 minutes)
+- `src/assets/` (logo, icons): `public, max-age=31536000` (one year)
+
+The script re-uploads everything on each deploy, then removes objects whose
+local files were deleted, then invalidates CloudFront as before.
+
+Why re-upload everything: `aws s3 sync` only uploads files that changed, and a
+header change alone does not count as a change. Without the re-upload, objects
+already in the bucket would keep no header, and the bug would stay for any file
+that has not changed since the last deploy.
+
+Why the uploads read from `src/` and not from S3: the copy-in-place approach
+from the colleague's note (`aws s3 cp s3://b/ s3://b/ --metadata-directive REPLACE`)
+replaces all metadata, including Content-Type. The script never copies S3
+objects onto themselves, so Content-Type is not touched. The AWS CLI sets it
+from each local file's extension at upload time, together with the cache header.
+
+I dry-ran the script with `aws` stubbed out (a shell function, no files written).
+It produced the three `aws s3` calls and the invalidation above, in order. I did
+not run it against S3 or CloudFront, so the live headers are still unchecked.
+
+## Colleague's note: what applied
+
+- **Applied:** short cache for HTML and JS, long cache for `assets/`, as you asked.
+- **Applied:** the header policy lives in the deploy script, not in shell history.
+- **Applied:** CloudFront invalidation after upload (the script already had it).
+- **Applied:** verify with `curl -I` and read back `content-type`, not just the 200 status.
+- **Applied in spirit:** the note says the old headers are also cached at the edge,
+  so the invalidation stays after the uploads.
+- **Not applicable:** the copy-onto-self `cp --metadata-directive REPLACE` loop and
+  the `binary/octet-stream` breakage. This script does not use it. The lesson
+  still holds: a flag that "only changes X" can rewrite more than X.
+- **Not applicable:** the per-extension Content-Type table. The CLI guesses the
+  type from the extension. If that guess is wrong on your machine, pass
+  `--content-type` explicitly per file type. I did not, because the verification
+  step below would show a wrong type.
+
+## Choices I made that you may want to change
+
+- **CSS** (`styles.css`) is in the short-cache group. You did not say where it
+  belongs. It is not under `assets/` and it changes like code, so it gets the short policy.
+- **One-year cache on `assets/` without renaming** means a changed `logo.png` will
+  not reach returning visitors for up to a year. If you edit an asset in place,
+  rename it (for example `logo.v2.png`). Otherwise use a shorter `max-age`.
+- **Full re-upload every deploy.** Fine for a site this size. It costs a few
+  requests per deploy.
+- **README** still says the problem is open ("Known problem" section). I did not
+  change it. Update it once the deploy is confirmed.
+
+## Not done
+
+- Nothing was deployed, published, or sent to any external service, as instructed.
+- No live check of headers. After your next deploy, run:
+
+```bash
+curl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/ | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'
+curl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/booking.js | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'
+curl -sI https://YOUR-SITE/assets/logo.png | grep -i -E 'content-type|cache-control'
+```
+
+Expected:
+
+- `/` → `content-type: text/html`, `cache-control: public, max-age=300`
+- `/booking.js` → `content-type` is `application/javascript` or `text/javascript`
+  (not `binary/octet-stream`), `cache-control: public, max-age=300`
+- `/assets/logo.png` → `content-type: image/png`, `cache-control: public, max-age=31536000`
+
+Then load the site in a browser that has not seen it before.
